1
00:00:23,192 --> 00:00:32,799
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I thought we could discuss authenticity as the... or the lack of authenticity is the main obstacle to understanding the dhamma.

2
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:43,126
[SPEAKER_02]: So, first of all, like, instead of describing authenticity in positive terms,

3
00:00:44,584 --> 00:00:55,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Can we come up with some way to describe how lack of authenticity would be most concretely manifested in people?

4
00:01:03,429 --> 00:01:03,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Like?

5
00:01:10,728 --> 00:01:18,231
[SPEAKER_02]: There's lying to others, of course, which is, like, even further into the rabbit hole of authenticity.

6
00:01:18,851 --> 00:01:20,652
[SPEAKER_02]: Inauthenticity, yeah.

7
00:01:21,292 --> 00:01:31,856
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you lie to others, it will be impossible to stop lying to yourself, which fundamentally is what lack of authenticity is.

8
00:01:31,956 --> 00:01:32,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Inauthenticity.

9
00:01:33,116 --> 00:01:34,297
[SPEAKER_02]: Lying to yourself, like,

10
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:43,862
[SPEAKER_02]: Things are assured in a way, but you insist in regarding them in another way, because it's more comfortable.

11
00:01:44,983 --> 00:01:49,164
[SPEAKER_02]: So, yeah, that would be one way.

12
00:01:52,385 --> 00:02:00,567
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there something that... Let's say somebody who hasn't found a way out of suffering.

13
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:23,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there a way to know that they are not being authentic, just based on how they go through life, how they feel?

14
00:02:23,653 --> 00:02:33,160
[SPEAKER_04]: Somebody can ask if they have an attitude of personal responsibility for everything they do, say, think and feel.

15
00:02:34,477 --> 00:02:39,241
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's an attitude that one can adopt and can develop.

16
00:02:40,502 --> 00:02:49,450
[SPEAKER_04]: And to the extent that one does not have that sense of full, complete responsibility, I would say someone is inauthentic.

17
00:02:50,251 --> 00:02:59,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but how would it more accurately, like, feel not to have that attitude versus having that attitude?

18
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:22,527
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, somebody, for example, might feel like a victim of circumstances, like the origin of my pain is coming from outside, external, and so the problem needs to be resolved out there, in an assumed external world, rather than on the level of one's own views and actions.

19
00:03:25,308 --> 00:03:27,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, so that would be, eh...

20
00:03:28,703 --> 00:03:30,044
[SPEAKER_02]: I was referring more to like...

21
00:03:31,065 --> 00:03:42,034
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as a result of this, that you're talking about now, a person would feel a sense of safety, but a sense of false safety.

22
00:03:42,375 --> 00:03:42,575
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

23
00:03:42,955 --> 00:03:53,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Because not acknowledging things, or not recognizing the truth of things, in the most general sense, not recognizing

24
00:03:56,125 --> 00:03:57,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Or acknowledge it the way things are.

25
00:03:58,867 --> 00:04:03,711
[SPEAKER_02]: Not in some mystical sense, but generally the state of affairs, let's say.

26
00:04:05,024 --> 00:04:08,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Results in a form of ignorance is bliss, as they say.

27
00:04:09,268 --> 00:04:09,909
[SPEAKER_04]: Uh-huh, yes.

28
00:04:10,670 --> 00:04:26,646
[SPEAKER_02]: So, practically speaking, that's why the the suttas are saying that recognizing dukkha can only result in two things, either noble search or madness, bewilderment, because

29
00:04:28,123 --> 00:04:42,230
[SPEAKER_02]: Recognizing dukkha is basically recognizing or acknowledging the state of affairs, not in an external sense, but internally, like your liability to lose things that are dear to you.

30
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:48,192
[SPEAKER_02]: Your liability to doubt, not knowing what the way out of suffering is.

31
00:04:49,133 --> 00:04:55,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Liability to uncertainty, to sickness, aging, and death.

32
00:04:56,652 --> 00:05:08,418
[SPEAKER_02]: So, these are things that, by not acknowledging them, you would feel a sense of relief, but it's an unfounded relief.

33
00:05:08,458 --> 00:05:20,203
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, what I'm getting at is that if a person doesn't feel a sense of... not like acute, kind of like, you know,

34
00:05:21,864 --> 00:05:29,493
[SPEAKER_02]: falling apart, sort of, constant anxiety, but just a subtle sense of unease, like something is not right.

35
00:05:30,855 --> 00:05:40,847
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it has to be either because they are an arahant or they are covering something up, because you are liable to sickness, aging and death.

36
00:05:42,251 --> 00:05:43,151
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you're narahant.

37
00:05:43,371 --> 00:05:47,112
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, that has to be recognized.

38
00:05:47,212 --> 00:06:06,638
[SPEAKER_02]: If you keep that recognition, if you're not covering it up, which is fundamentally like what authenticity is, not covering those fundamental liabilities up, then, well, it's gonna have to feel... there's gonna have to be some degree of dissonance there, some degree of...

39
00:06:08,637 --> 00:06:09,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, discomforts.

40
00:06:09,997 --> 00:06:15,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's one of the ways to measure lack of authenticity.

41
00:06:15,358 --> 00:06:20,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you have the sense that everything will be all right.

42
00:06:22,099 --> 00:06:23,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Things will just turn out.

43
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:24,600
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have a reason to believe.

44
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:26,980
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have a justification why you believe that.

45
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:33,241
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just like, I just live life as if things are going to be all right in the end.

46
00:06:34,001 --> 00:06:36,162
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's covering up a fundamental

47
00:06:38,774 --> 00:06:41,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Perhaps we can find more ways in which

48
00:06:48,256 --> 00:06:50,417
[SPEAKER_04]: Inauthenticity manifests.

49
00:06:51,017 --> 00:07:17,563
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, for example, if if one finds justification for certain action or views because the majority thinks like that, right, or because you belong to a certain group, or a society holds certain values like this, and then just uncritically taking that over can be a way of of having that false sense of safety and security by just doing what everybody else is doing.

50
00:07:18,623 --> 00:07:41,809
[SPEAKER_04]: So, authenticity implies stepping away from that, which means that you're going to find yourself more on your own and that is experientially unpleasant, because one's ignorance or doubt about what is right, what is wrong, what are going to be the consequences of my actions and so on, that's going to be highlighted if you are not just

51
00:07:42,869 --> 00:07:52,235
[SPEAKER_04]: Casually moving along with what an anonymous they or a crowd or a herd happens to be doing.

52
00:07:52,476 --> 00:08:06,565
[SPEAKER_04]: That's another way, which doesn't mean that one cannot associate with other people, externally, objectively, but we're talking about an inner attitude of not just doing something because a certain group or a certain tradition

53
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,962
[SPEAKER_04]: tells you that that's good to do.

54
00:08:10,442 --> 00:08:12,965
[SPEAKER_04]: What needs to be more critical than that?

55
00:08:14,326 --> 00:08:32,502
[SPEAKER_02]: And recognizing, even if... even if you follow... it's not like I will not do anything, because in the end, you would be trusting yourself, still, when you go... when you... if you just assume that by not following anyone, you're already being authentic, that that wouldn't be the case, because

56
00:08:33,381 --> 00:08:37,605
[SPEAKER_02]: You might still be covering up the fact that you yourself are still liable to doubt.

57
00:08:37,825 --> 00:08:39,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you're not sure what's what's right.

58
00:08:40,287 --> 00:08:44,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not enough to just dismiss all authority authorities, for example.

59
00:08:44,571 --> 00:08:45,953
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a fundamental recognition.

60
00:08:45,993 --> 00:08:46,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, no, no.

61
00:08:47,054 --> 00:08:51,258
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether I agree with others or not, I don't know what is correct.

62
00:08:51,578 --> 00:08:53,199
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I will be agreeing with something.

63
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:55,121
[SPEAKER_02]: It's inevitable.

64
00:08:55,421 --> 00:08:55,882
[SPEAKER_02]: But then,

65
00:08:57,862 --> 00:09:08,672
[SPEAKER_00]: The underlying problem can only be fixed by recognizing that liability to doubt, not by finding ways to stop recognizing it so that it stops hurting.

66
00:09:09,433 --> 00:09:20,543
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why it's... the dhamma goes against the grain, basically, because in order to recognize... in order to work towards fixing a problem, you have to keep it in sight.

67
00:09:21,383 --> 00:09:22,444
[SPEAKER_02]: And keeping it in sight

68
00:09:23,644 --> 00:09:27,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Metaphorically speaking, in this case, it's not going to be pleasant.

69
00:09:27,269 --> 00:09:29,753
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you have a any kind of illness...

70
00:09:31,454 --> 00:09:51,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Going through a life-threatening illness, in this case, which is ignorance, like not understanding the Four Noble Truths, should be considered like the worst of all illnesses, because it is the thing that subjects you to the to the worst type of pain of, you know, rebirth and sickness, aging and death, even in this very life.

71
00:09:51,581 --> 00:09:55,442
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any kind of sickness that you could get,

72
00:09:56,909 --> 00:10:04,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, bodily sickness is kind of secondary to the fundamental sickness of not knowing the way out of suffering.

73
00:10:04,653 --> 00:10:09,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only way that any worldly thing would be able to affect you.

74
00:10:09,475 --> 00:10:18,900
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, keeping that in sight, it's like, you can either forget that you have terminal cancer and... and, like,

75
00:10:20,868 --> 00:10:33,154
[SPEAKER_02]: Kind of... not even like you you were told and you decided to forget about it, but like you have a sense that it could be, but you kind of... you don't go to the doctor to ask.

76
00:10:33,474 --> 00:10:35,896
[SPEAKER_00]: You just decide to live

77
00:10:37,182 --> 00:10:42,387
[SPEAKER_00]: The rest of your time, whatever that is, in the comfort of just not having to think about that.

78
00:10:43,127 --> 00:10:45,910
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what more like what inauthenticity is.

79
00:10:45,970 --> 00:10:49,132
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like you knew it and then you decided to ignore it.

80
00:10:49,493 --> 00:10:56,378
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like you have a vague sense that there's something there, but you're refusing to acknowledge it.

81
00:10:56,458 --> 00:10:56,819
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

82
00:10:57,470 --> 00:11:00,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Like in a room, you might have, you know, a dark corner of the room.

83
00:11:01,733 --> 00:11:07,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessarily in sight, but it's there, and like, it's the dirt is building up in that corner.

84
00:11:09,197 --> 00:11:13,800
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and you postpone actually going there and cleaning it.

85
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:16,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what, that's more what it's like.

86
00:11:16,822 --> 00:11:21,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all this external

87
00:11:23,691 --> 00:11:34,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Conformity to tradition and all that sort of stuff, those are just like the different mechanisms that you could use to cover up that liability to uncertainty.

88
00:11:35,017 --> 00:11:50,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if the group says something, and you go along with it, well, that provides that kind of... that distraction from having to question, like, well, whether or not they are right,

89
00:11:51,388 --> 00:12:00,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Is the problem that I am addressing through this, whatever it might be, even the right problem to focus on in the first place?

90
00:12:00,974 --> 00:12:20,127
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you recognize there's so much stuff to doubt, to question, that in the end, any sort of... any sort of, like, fixed first principle that you start from could be could be put into question.

91
00:12:20,147 --> 00:12:20,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

92
00:12:22,010 --> 00:12:29,137
[SPEAKER_00]: That can't be something that you find safety in, if you are recognizing it rightly.

93
00:12:29,297 --> 00:12:33,342
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be at least somewhat unsettling.

94
00:12:33,382 --> 00:12:33,922
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be.

95
00:12:35,384 --> 00:12:38,307
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

96
00:12:38,347 --> 00:12:45,574
[SPEAKER_04]: So, a desire for, let's say, distraction, or doing things in order to feel comfortable,

97
00:12:47,374 --> 00:12:55,018
[SPEAKER_04]: By definition, it means that there are things that one wants to distract oneself from, or that there is an underlying discomfort that one doesn't want to face.

98
00:12:56,218 --> 00:13:08,904
[SPEAKER_04]: So, rather than spending time to organize one's pleasures and comforts, like on the inner, let's say, emotional or psychological level, not like having a roof over your head and some clothes to wear, that's quite necessary, right?

99
00:13:08,924 --> 00:13:11,785
[SPEAKER_04]: So, we're talking on another level.

100
00:13:12,686 --> 00:13:12,926
[SPEAKER_04]: Then,

101
00:13:14,226 --> 00:13:17,568
[SPEAKER_04]: Someone who is authentic would recognize that and turn around.

102
00:13:17,889 --> 00:13:27,174
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, what is it that I'm right now not wanting to see, through my desire to be distracted or to dissolve in a group and things like that.

103
00:13:27,734 --> 00:13:29,656
[SPEAKER_04]: And that has got to become the habit.

104
00:13:31,577 --> 00:13:36,240
[SPEAKER_04]: If one wants to move towards being authentic, because if there truly wouldn't be anything

105
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:47,626
[SPEAKER_04]: If that disquiet wouldn't be there, then it would not be necessary to seek sensual pleasures or to find that, let's say, full sense of safety, because all that would be unnecessary.

106
00:13:47,646 --> 00:13:48,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

107
00:13:48,186 --> 00:13:52,348
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can ask yourself, like, so, if I don't do anything, will I suffer?

108
00:13:52,448 --> 00:14:02,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, in the sense of, like, I just suspend all my engagements that I might have accumulated over time, and I just don't commit to anything, basically.

109
00:14:03,594 --> 00:14:04,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Will I

110
00:14:05,042 --> 00:14:07,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Will that uncertainty start to show up?

111
00:14:07,666 --> 00:14:10,170
[SPEAKER_02]: If it does, it's not because, like...

112
00:14:11,654 --> 00:14:17,036
[SPEAKER_02]: The uncertainty is a result of you not having the engagements anymore.

113
00:14:17,637 --> 00:14:20,758
[SPEAKER_02]: It's that these things were covering up the uncertainty.

114
00:14:21,298 --> 00:14:22,919
[SPEAKER_02]: But often, it's seen the other way around.

115
00:14:22,959 --> 00:14:29,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if I start to practice restraint or renunciation, I am suffering because I'm restraining myself now.

116
00:14:30,482 --> 00:14:31,182
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not like that.

117
00:14:31,262 --> 00:14:35,364
[SPEAKER_02]: It's that unpleasantness that comes when you don't give in.

118
00:14:36,388 --> 00:14:42,833
[SPEAKER_02]: That is what was already there, kind of lurking, and you were sweeping it under the rug.

119
00:14:43,293 --> 00:14:43,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

120
00:14:43,574 --> 00:14:46,236
[SPEAKER_00]: By distracting yourself with pleasure.

121
00:14:46,616 --> 00:14:46,796
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

122
00:14:46,816 --> 00:14:52,841
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not so much a movement towards something positive, say, that one doesn't have right now.

123
00:14:53,101 --> 00:14:54,522
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a sort of... it's inverted.

124
00:14:54,582 --> 00:14:57,204
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like an uncovering of something which is already there.

125
00:14:57,364 --> 00:14:57,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

126
00:14:57,624 --> 00:15:01,807
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop doing certain things, most importantly, like through virtue and sensuousness.

127
00:15:02,087 --> 00:15:03,288
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop doing certain things,

128
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:08,579
[SPEAKER_02]: So that what's already there can become clear.

129
00:15:09,481 --> 00:15:12,988
[SPEAKER_02]: So, rather than seeing it more as...

130
00:15:14,834 --> 00:15:16,194
[SPEAKER_02]: I've heard that similarly used before.

131
00:15:16,214 --> 00:15:21,216
[SPEAKER_02]: You have a glass with water in it, and it has mud in it.

132
00:15:22,016 --> 00:15:26,517
[SPEAKER_00]: And you need to stop shaking it, so that the mud will go to the bottom.

133
00:15:27,137 --> 00:15:31,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But that would usually be used in the context of focusing the mind and concentration techniques.

134
00:15:31,978 --> 00:15:36,819
[SPEAKER_02]: But in this case, it is exactly what you're doing, but in terms of your actions.

135
00:15:37,339 --> 00:15:44,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop stirring it up through greed, aversion, and delusion, so that it all sinks to the bottom, and you can start

136
00:15:45,402 --> 00:15:48,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing that dirt where it is.

137
00:15:50,046 --> 00:16:07,885
[SPEAKER_02]: So, and it's... that's where you will start to see what that... those underlying tendencies, where the mind kind of... in and of itself, it just needs or has certain pressures, certain inclinations that it has to give in to,

138
00:16:09,071 --> 00:16:12,732
[SPEAKER_02]: And, eh... you would only start recognizing that if you stop feeding them.

139
00:16:13,893 --> 00:16:28,178
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... that is why authenticity is important to understanding the dhamma, because the things that need to be abandoned and addressed and overcome are things that

140
00:16:29,458 --> 00:16:32,439
[SPEAKER_00]: are very easy to ignore, are things that are very easy to overlook.

141
00:16:33,059 --> 00:16:36,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the default tendency is not to see them.

142
00:16:37,301 --> 00:16:43,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, what Greed, Aversion, and Delusion are, they're not these objects or

143
00:16:45,643 --> 00:16:47,404
[SPEAKER_04]: And there are entities in front of you.

144
00:16:47,584 --> 00:16:47,884
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah.

145
00:16:47,904 --> 00:16:51,806
[SPEAKER_02]: They're not like direct perceptions that you can get.

146
00:16:52,406 --> 00:16:56,268
[SPEAKER_02]: It is all on that same level of the things that you're covering up.

147
00:16:57,229 --> 00:17:03,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why lack of authenticity would essentially make you incapable of recognizing your own defilements.

148
00:17:04,752 --> 00:17:10,855
[SPEAKER_02]: Because everything you do is already based on them, like way down the line.

149
00:17:11,835 --> 00:17:19,017
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how can you possibly see the source of the river, to put it that way?

150
00:17:19,137 --> 00:17:27,739
[SPEAKER_00]: The source of the pollution, you're already engaged with the dirt that comes way, way past it.

151
00:17:27,799 --> 00:17:35,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, that's why it's not like a philosophical concept only.

152
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It has very acute significance to

153
00:17:40,447 --> 00:17:46,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognizing what craving is, because you can't abandon craving if you haven't recognized it to begin with.

154
00:17:48,210 --> 00:17:53,852
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so people need to recognize that to actually come to terms with craving is going to be unpleasant.

155
00:17:54,112 --> 00:17:55,212
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

156
00:17:55,252 --> 00:17:57,293
[SPEAKER_04]: But can we make it more explicit why that is so?

157
00:17:57,893 --> 00:18:07,577
[SPEAKER_04]: For example, like what is the most dear or cherished thing that is most difficult to give up?

158
00:18:19,038 --> 00:18:27,086
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, what is going to be challenged, let's say inwardly, for lack of a better word, if if one is come to terms with craving?

159
00:18:28,047 --> 00:18:30,930
[SPEAKER_04]: Or what is it that is fueled and maintained by craving?

160
00:18:46,187 --> 00:18:53,676
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, what I'm trying to get at is that it is one's own identity that at some point is going to be challenged by all this.

161
00:18:55,118 --> 00:18:57,120
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, perhaps you could say that

162
00:18:58,633 --> 00:19:03,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Safety and security, yeah, it begins by outer comfort and distraction and stuff like that.

163
00:19:03,696 --> 00:19:08,919
[SPEAKER_04]: But, fundamentally, one sort of takes refuge in oneself, as you sort of said already somewhat earlier.

164
00:19:09,399 --> 00:19:15,042
[SPEAKER_04]: But, that needs to go as well, if one wants to gain the true refuge in terms of dhamma.

165
00:19:15,863 --> 00:19:22,406
[SPEAKER_04]: So, one's own identity comes up for, let's say, in investigation as well.

166
00:19:22,687 --> 00:19:24,407
[SPEAKER_02]: If you remove, like, the

167
00:19:26,055 --> 00:19:29,657
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no outer ways that you were covering up that recognition.

168
00:19:29,677 --> 00:19:42,084
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically impossible that you'll get to that core, which is your identity, if you still maintain the things that are a result of that identity, let's say.

169
00:19:42,584 --> 00:19:49,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the sensual, perverse, distracted actions, the looted, careless actions.

170
00:19:50,468 --> 00:19:51,609
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no way that you will see

171
00:19:52,809 --> 00:19:56,912
[SPEAKER_00]: The source of where that's coming from, which is the identity, if you're still maintaining it.

172
00:19:57,672 --> 00:20:09,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why authenticity is not just a choice of, like, taking up a certain attitude as you would just, like, change your mindset or something.

173
00:20:10,360 --> 00:20:12,781
[SPEAKER_00]: It is bound with the choices that you make.

174
00:20:12,881 --> 00:20:18,525
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, there are certain choices that you can't make and be fully authentic at the same time.

175
00:20:19,105 --> 00:20:21,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And those are the things that go against the precepts.

176
00:20:21,326 --> 00:20:25,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the things that an arahant cannot do, most fundamentally.

177
00:20:26,109 --> 00:20:34,533
[SPEAKER_00]: The five things that an arahant cannot do is like killing, stealing, lying, sexual intercourse, and taking intoxicants.

178
00:20:35,553 --> 00:20:38,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Any of those things, by doing them, you are being inauthentic.

179
00:20:40,572 --> 00:20:55,776
[SPEAKER_00]: To a varying degree, of course, but fundamentally, if you're training yourself, those things need to go for you to uncover, start uncovering the stuff that you cover up by doing them.

180
00:21:00,817 --> 00:21:09,259
[SPEAKER_04]: And now, if someone has been following this so far, and a sense of anxiety begins to arise, then what?

